{"subjectId":"sql","id":"cross-self-join","title":"CROSS JOIN & SELF JOIN","group":"Joins & Set Operations","order":28,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A cross join pairs every row with every row; a self join matches a table with itself. Both are ordinary joins used in less ordinary ways.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-grid-dots\"></i>CROSS JOIN: every combination</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE sizes  (size TEXT);\nCREATE TABLE colors (color TEXT);\nINSERT INTO sizes  VALUES ('S'), ('M'), ('L');\nINSERT INTO colors VALUES ('Black'), ('White');\n\nSELECT s.size, c.color\nFROM sizes s CROSS JOIN colors c\nORDER BY c.color, s.size;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>size | color\n-----+------\nL    | Black\nM    | Black\nS    | Black\nL    | White\nM    | White\nS    | White</code></pre></figure>\n<p>3 × 2 = 6 rows. Real uses: generating all product variants, a calendar × store grid to report zero-sales days, or combining a single-row settings table with every row.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- every department × every month of Q1, so empty months still appear\nWITH months(m) AS (VALUES ('2024-01'), ('2024-02'), ('2024-03'))\nSELECT d.name, m.m AS month\nFROM departments d CROSS JOIN months m\nWHERE d.dept_id &lt;= 2\nORDER BY d.name, month;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name        | month\n------------+--------\nEngineering | 2024-01\nEngineering | 2024-02\nEngineering | 2024-03\nSales       | 2024-01\nSales       | 2024-02\nSales       | 2024-03</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Cross joins grow multiplicatively: 10 000 × 10 000 rows = 100 million. An accidental one (missing join condition) is a classic cause of runaway queries.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hierarchy\"></i>SELF JOIN: employees and their managers</h3>\n<p><code>employees.manager_id</code> points at another row of <code>employees</code>. Join the table to itself under two aliases — one playing <em>employee</em>, one playing <em>manager</em>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT e.name AS employee, m.name AS manager\nFROM employees e\nLEFT JOIN employees m ON m.emp_id = e.manager_id\nORDER BY e.emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>employee | manager\n---------+--------\nAsha     | NULL\nRavi     | Asha\nMeera    | Ravi\nKaran    | Ravi\nPriya    | Asha\nArjun    | Priya\nNeha     | Priya\nVikram   | Asha\nSara     | Vikram\nDev      | Asha</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>LEFT JOIN</code> keeps Asha, who has no manager. With an inner join she would vanish.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- employees who earn more than their manager (a top interview question)\nSELECT e.name AS employee, e.salary, m.name AS manager, m.salary AS manager_salary\nFROM employees e\nJOIN employees m ON m.emp_id = e.manager_id\nWHERE e.salary &gt; m.salary;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>employee | salary | manager | manager_salary\n---------+--------+---------+---------------\nSara     | 85000  | Vikram  | 80000</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-exchange\"></i>Self join to compare rows: pairs</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- pairs of employees in the same department (each pair once)\nSELECT a.name AS first, b.name AS second, a.dept_id\nFROM employees a\nJOIN employees b ON b.dept_id = a.dept_id AND a.emp_id &lt; b.emp_id\nWHERE a.dept_id IN (2, 3)\nORDER BY a.dept_id, a.emp_id, b.emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>first  | second | dept_id\n-------+--------+--------\nPriya  | Arjun  | 2\nPriya  | Neha   | 2\nArjun  | Neha   | 2\nVikram | Sara   | 3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>a.emp_id &lt; b.emp_id</code> removes self-pairs (Priya–Priya) and mirrored duplicates (Priya–Arjun and Arjun–Priya). <code>&lt;&gt;</code> would keep both mirrors.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is a self join and when is it used?</summary><div><p>A join of a table with itself using two aliases. Used for hierarchical data stored as a parent pointer (employee → manager, category → parent category), for comparing rows within the same table (pairs, duplicates, consecutive events), and for “earns more than manager”-style questions.</p></div></details></section>"}